This page continues the previous one (p.219), which set up four bigraded complexes over a commutative ring A attached to an A-module M:
- (1) the de Rham complex Sym*M ⊗ Λ*M = Ω•_{Sym M/A};
- (2) the Koszul complex on Sym*M ⊗ Λ*M, with δ: Sym^p⊗Λ^q → Sym^{p+1}⊗Λ^{q-1};
- (3) the divided-power de Rham complex Γ*M ⊗ Λ*M, with d(x^{[i]}) = x^{[i-1]}dx;
- (4) the divided-power Koszul complex on Γ*M ⊗ Λ*M.

The page then establishes the following.

(i) Structure. All four are differential graded algebras, functorial in (A,M). For fixed A they turn direct sums into tensor products: Sym(M⊕N) = Sym M⊗Sym N, Γ(M⊕N) = ΓM⊗ΓN, Λ(M⊕N) = ΛM⊗ΛN, compatibly with the differentials. This is the exponential property, a Künneth structure. They also commute with filtered colimits.

(ii) The canonical map Sym*M → Γ*M. This algebra map, x ↦ x^{[1]}, induces a DGA map C*_DR(Sym M) → C*_{DR,p.d.}(Γ M) compatible with d. It is an isomorphism when A is a Q-algebra (the note says 'car 0'). So in that case the cohomology of (1) is computed by that of (3).

The computation of (3) for M flat goes in three steps:
- reduce to M free of finite type by Lazard's theorem (a flat module is a filtered colimit of free finite modules);
- reduce by Künneth to rank 1;
- in rank 1, Γ(x) → Γ(x)dx, x^{[n]} ↦ x^{[n-1]}dx, is surjective with kernel A.

This gives H^0 = A and H^i = 0 for i > 0 over any base ring: the PD Poincaré lemma.

(iii) The same map Sym → Γ is compatible with the Koszul differentials δ. So, by the same procedure, the homology of (4) reduces to that of (2) (in the Q-algebra case). The ordinary Koszul complex (2) is handled by the same Lazard + Künneth + rank-1 method. In rank 1 it is A[x] → A[x], multiplication by x, which is injective with cokernel A.

(iv) The page starts to show that δ in (2) and (4) is a special case of the general Koszul complex. Given a commutative ring S, an S-module 𝓜 and a linear form φ: 𝓜 → S, there is a unique antiderivation of Λ_S𝓜 extending φ, and it squares to zero. This is continued on p.221 with S = Sym M or Γ M, 𝓜 = M⊗S, and φ given by multiplication via M ≅ S^1.

Overall purpose: to set up the PD de Rham complex of Γ M as the correct analogue, in any characteristic, of the polynomial de Rham complex. It is 'dual' to the ordinary Koszul complex. Over char p the remaining two complexes (ordinary de Rham (1) and PD Koszul (4)) are not acyclic, which is where the Cartier operation mentioned on p.219 would enter. This is background for crystalline cohomology / the 1976 IHÉS talk.
